{"surface": "inspector", "title": "Full text", "html": "<header class=\"hero\"><p class=\"kicker\">Full text</p><h2 class=\"title\">benzinga.com</h2><p class=\"ctx\"><a href=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/earnings/26/11/63359678/why-is-omeros-stock-trading-lower-tuesday\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">benzinga.com/markets/earnings/26/11/63359678/why-is-omeros-stock-trading-lower-tuesday</a> · read Nov 19, 2026</p></header><div class=\"page md\" data-md=\"# Why Is Omeros Stock Trading Lower Tuesday?\n\n**Omeros Corp.** [(NASDAQ:OMER)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/OMER) stock fell sharply Tuesday after the biotechnology company reported mixed third-quarter 2026 results, with adjusted earnings exceeding Wall Street expectations but [revenue falling short](https://www.benzinga.com/pressreleases/26/11/b63321207/omeros-corporation-reports-third-quarter-2026-financial-results).\n\nThe company reported adjusted earnings of 6 cents per share, beating the consensus [estimate of 3 cents](https://www.benzinga.com/earnings).\n\nRevenue reached \\$35.93 million, missing the consensus estimate of \\$37.27 million.\n\n## Third-Quarter Financial Results\n\nOmeros reported a net loss of \\$57.4 million, or 79 cents per share, compared with a net loss of \\$30.9 million, or 47 cents per share, a year earlier.\n\nThe third-quarter results included a noncash loss of \\$62 million, primarily related to mark-to-market adjustments of a derivative associated with unsecured convertible notes due in 2029.\n\nAdjusted net income rose to \\$4.7 million, or 6 cents per share, from \\$1.8 million, or 2 cents per share, in the second quarter.\n\nOperating income reached \\$4.5 million, compared with an operating loss of \\$26.4 million a year earlier.\n\nOmeros ended September with \\$95.3 million in cash and short-term investments. The company generated \\$13.3 million in positive operating cash flow during the quarter.\n\nOmeros repurchased \\$30.5 million in principal amount of its 2029 convertible notes for \\$60.2 million, excluding accrued interest. The transactions reduced the outstanding principal to approximately \\$40.3 million and eliminated \\$8.6 million in scheduled interest payments.\n\nTogether with earlier stock repurchases, the transactions reduced the number of outstanding or potentially issuable shares by approximately 5.8 million year to date.\n\n## Yartemlea Sales Continue to Grow\n\nYartemlea (narsoplimab-wuug), the first and only approved treatment for stem cell transplant-associated thrombotic microangiopathy (TA-TMA), continued to generate sequential revenue growth.\n\nTA-TMA is a potentially fatal complication of stem cell transplantation.\n\nYartemlea’s gross revenue increased 30% to \\$41.8 million in the third quarter from \\$32.2 million in the second quarter.\n\nNet revenue rose 26% to \\$35.9 million from \\$28.5 million in the prior quarter. However, the gross-to-net adjustment increased to 14% from 11.5%.\n\nThe company attributed the higher gross-to-net adjustment to increased purchases by entities covered by the 340B Drug Pricing Program, reflecting broader customer adoption and increased outpatient use.\n\nYartemlea’s New Technology Add-on Payment designation took effect Oct. 1. Eligible hospitals can receive up to \\$287,079 in additional Medicare reimbursement per eligible inpatient case involving the drug.\n\n**Read Also: [Omeros Transplant Drug Could Be Bigger than Expected, Says Analyst](https://www.benzinga.com/analyst-stock-ratings/upgrades/26/09/61930111/omeros-transplant-drug-could-be-bigger-than-expected-says-analyst)**\n\n## Omeros Plans to Expand Yartemlea’s Clinical Development\n\nOmeros expects to support two investigator-initiated or company co-sponsored [studies evaluating Yartemlea](https://www.benzinga.com/biotech).\n\nOne study will evaluate the drug in patients with hyperinflammatory acute respiratory distress syndrome. The other will assess its ability to prevent TA-TMA in high-risk [pediatric transplant patients](https://www.benzinga.com/health-care).\n\nBoth open-label, single-arm studies will use historical controls. Each study aims to enroll approximately 50 to 60 patients.\n\nOmeros expects the studies to begin in late 2026 or early 2027, subject to institutional review board and ethics committee approvals.\n\nThe company is also exploring additional applications for Yartemlea in conditions involving blood vessel damage, lectin pathway activation or inflammation associated with blood clotting.\n\n## Novo Collaboration Offers Additional Growth Opportunities\n\nOmeros said **Novo Nordisk A/S** [(NYSE:NVO)](https://www.benzinga.com/quote/NVO) remains on track to initiate a Phase 3 trial of zaltenibart, an antibody it acquired from Omeros in 2025.\n\nOmeros remains eligible for more than \\$1.8 billion in development and commercial milestone payments, including up to \\$100 million in near-term payments, plus tiered royalties on net sales.\n\n## European Regulatory Setback\n\nIn October, the European Medicines Agency’s Committee for Medicinal Products for Human Use issued a negative opinion on Omeros’ application to market [narsoplimab for TA-TMA](https://www.benzinga.com/trading-ideas/movers/26/10/62405950/omeros-stock-sinks-on-negative-eu-opinion-for-narsoplimab-compassionate-use-program-ends).\n\nThe decision followed a re-examination of the committee’s original [negative opinion in June](https://www.benzinga.com/news/health-care/26/06/60130423/fda-approved-europe-unconvinced-omeros-stem-cell-transplant-treatment-hits-regulatory-divide).\n\nEuropean regulators previously raised concerns about the study design, insufficient evidence of the drug’s effectiveness and limited pediatric data.\n\nOmeros also announced plans in October to end its compassionate-use program amid rising U.S. demand. The company is working to provide fully reimbursed, named-patient access in Europe and other regions where permitted.\n\nMeanwhile, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration approved Yartemlea in December 2025 to treat TA-TMA in adults and children ages 2 and older.\n\n**OMER Price Action:** Omeros shares were down 17.81% at \\$13.71 at the time of publication on Tuesday. Trading volume reached 2.57 million shares, about 65.5% above the 100-day average of 1.55 million shares, [according to Benzinga Pro data](https://www.benzinga.com/pro/register-3).\n\n*Image via Shutterstock*\n\n**Read Also: [Chuck Schumer Slams Trump Over Plan to Cut Food Stamps for 6 Million Americans: &#x27;Empty Dinner Plates Across America&#x27;](https://www.benzinga.com/markets/economic-data/26/11/63332808/chuck-schumer-slams-trump-over-plan-to-cut-food-stamps-for-6-million-americans-empty-dinner-plates-across-america)**\" data-base=\"https://www.benzinga.com/markets/earnings/26/11/63359678/why-is-omeros-stock-trading-lower-tuesday\"></div><p class=\"raw\"><a class=\"rawlink\" data-go=\"raw:src:108\">Full details</a></p>", "wide": true}